<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Distinguish a connection, a resource and a symbol, and change databases deliberately.
</div>

**You need:** the client packages and the Opal login from
[Getting started](book-00-start.html). This chapter uses `omop.gibleed` and
`omop.synthea`.

## Start this chapter

```{r book-local-server, include = FALSE}
# Outputs come from a local DataSHIELD server holding the same resources as the Opal in the text.
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
```

```{r chapter-setup, eval = FALSE}
library(dsOMOPClient)
conns <- ds.omop.login("https://localhost:8443", user = "administrator",
  password = "password", resource = "omop.gibleed", server = "site")$conns
```

```{r chapter-setup-local, include = FALSE}
conns <- book_connect("gibleed")$conns
```

## Three names with different jobs

A **connection** is a logged-in DataSHIELD session. A **resource** describes how
the server opens a database. A **symbol** is an object name in that session.
Here `site` names the connection, `omop.gibleed` selects the resource and `omop`
names the attached database handle.

```{r handle}
DSI::datashield.symbols(conns)
```

Under `site`, `omop` is the private handle that `ds.omop.login()` created. Later,
`D_people` will name a table created by a recipe, rather than a database handle.

## Use a handle explicitly

```{r explicit-handle}
ds.omop.table.stats("person", symbol = "omop", datasources = conns)
```

The request goes to GiBleed through `omop`: its person count is in the
2,690–2,694 band. Most dsOMOP calls default to `symbol = "omop"`, so you can
omit it when you have just one attached database.
`ds.omop.server.version()` is different: it reports the server's installed and
available methods, independently of any database handle.

## Switch resources

Release the attached resource, then attach another one through the same login:

```{r switch-to-numeric}
ds.omop.disconnect(datasources = conns)
ds.omop.connect(resource = "omop.synthea", datasources = conns)
ds.omop.table.stats("person", datasources = conns)
```

The person band is now 25–29, containing Synthea27Nj's 28 people. The handle is
still called `omop` even though its database changed. Switching does not combine
the two populations.

```{r switch-back}
ds.omop.disconnect(datasources = conns)
ds.omop.connect(resource = "omop.gibleed", datasources = conns)
ds.omop.table.stats("person", datasources = conns)
```

The 2,690–2,694 band confirms that GiBleed is attached again. Choosing the
resource explicitly prevents running a lab analysis against its empty numeric fields.

## Exercise 1: keep two handles in one session

Attach `omop.synthea` as `labs`, keeping `omop` attached to GiBleed. List the
names and count people through each. Does attaching `labs` combine them?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r two-handles}
ds.omop.connect(resource = "omop.synthea", symbol = "labs", datasources = conns)
DSI::datashield.symbols(conns)
```

`labs` and `omop` are two distinct handles in the same session.

```{r two-counts}
ds.omop.table.stats("person", stats = "persons", symbol = "omop", datasources = conns)
ds.omop.table.stats("person", stats = "persons", symbol = "labs", datasources = conns)
ds.omop.disconnect(symbol = "labs", datasources = conns)
```

`omop` gives 2690 and `labs` gives 25. Each is a separate protected count; the
populations were not combined. Disconnecting `labs` leaves `omop` available.

</details>

## Exercise 2: diagnose a wrong name

Ask for a person count through `missing_handle`. What needs fixing: the table
name or the handle name?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

<!-- expected-refusal:start -->
```{r unknown-handle, error = TRUE}
ds.omop.table.stats("person", symbol = "missing_handle", datasources = conns)
```
<!-- expected-refusal:end -->

The message identifies a missing session handle. It says nothing about whether
the database has a person table. Use `omop`, or attach a resource under the name
you intend to query.

</details>

## End the session

```{r logout}
ds.omop.disconnect(datasources = conns)
DSI::datashield.logout(conns)
```

Disconnect releases the database handle; logout ends the DataSHIELD session.
The next chapter logs in again.

## Recap

A resource selects a database, a connection carries requests, and a symbol names
a private server object. Check the selected resource before analysing it.

**Next:** [3. Exploring the CDM](book-03-explore.html).
